From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan: count frames when they reach the screen

The frametime was the CPU time between two vkQueuePresentKHR calls. On a
presentation engine that holds a present until its target and repeats it
(PortareOS mesa-003, mesa-005), that is how long the application took
per frame, not what the display showed.

Where the device offers VK_GOOGLE_display_timing and the swapchain is on
a VK_KHR_display surface, a present is now counted when its record says
when it reached the screen, and the interval between two such times is
the frame's display interval. If the application enabled the extension,
the layer only observes: presents go down unchanged and it copies what
the application's own vkGetPastPresentationTimingGOOGLE returns, since
a record is handed out once and the application times frames from it.
If not, the layer enables it, tags each present with its own ID and
target 0, and reads everything after each present; the application does
not see the extension.

A present is counted once, in order: when its record comes (ok), when a
later one's comes first (skipped), after 500 ms (timeout), or when the
swapchain is destroyed or falls back to counting at present (end). It
falls back for good at a present that is untagged, shared with another
swapchain, or reuses a pending ID, and when a present times out without
the application having read since. update_hud_info_with_frametime is
split into a periodic part, run at every present, and a frame part, run
per counted present.

The log replaces frametime with application_interval_ms and
display_interval_ms and adds present_id, target_error_ms and record;
missing values are empty. The summary's percentiles use display
intervals when there are any and count the timeouts and missing records.
The HUD shows the mean display interval, or -- when none came.

A present split from one of several swapchains used to carry the whole
VkPresentTimesInfoGOOGLE, so every swapchain got the first entry; each
now gets its own.

Measured on SM8550 (Retroid Pocket Nova) against a DRM/DPU flip trace:
every display interval equal to the trace's vblank timestamps to the
microsecond, in RetroArch at variable (1201 frames) and fixed refresh
(3589) and in a test client. The unpatched layer's frametimes missed
all 19 frames held for three refreshes in the fixed refresh run.
Present hook median 204 us against 200-214 us unpatched.

Signed-off-by: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
